      <h1>Lithium Battery Warranty and Failure Analysis — What the Claim Needs</h1>
      <div class="takeaways"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A warranty claim is an evidence exercise: the failure mode, the operating history and the pack condition decide the outcome. A policy that excludes the actual cause — or cannot trace the pack — is worth less than its headline years.</div>

<h2>Read the warranty as a specification</h2>
<p>Check what is warranted (capacity, not just "the battery"), the throughput or cycle limit, the operating window, the exclusions and the remedy. A 10-year warranty that excludes cycling beyond 1,000 cycles is a 1,000-cycle warranty with a long calendar tail.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Question to ask</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Capacity guarantee</td><td>What percentage, measured how, at what temperature and C-rate?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cycle limit</td><td>Is the warranty pro-rata after a threshold?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operating window</td><td>Temperature, SOC, charge rate and depth of discharge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclusions</td><td>Water, vibration, unauthorised BMS changes, reverse polarity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Repair, replacement, credit or refund — and who pays freight?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Common failure modes and their evidence</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Failure</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Capacity fade faster than specified</td><td>High temperature, high C-rate, deep cycling</td><td>BMS logs, temperature history, cycle count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sudden death / open circuit</td><td>Weld or busbar failure</td><td>Resistance map, cross-section, inspection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swelling</td><td>Overcharge, over-discharge, gas generation</td><td>Voltage log, BMS thresholds, charge profile</td></tr>
<tr><td>Imbalance</td><td>Cell mismatch, faulty balancer</td><td>Cell voltage history, balancing current</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMS failure</td><td>Moisture, vibration, overvoltage</td><td>Enclosure inspection, event log</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>What a claim should include</h2>
<p>The pack serial number, the purchase and installation date, the BMS event log, the operating conditions, photographs of the installation and the failed part, and a capacity or resistance test result. Without the log, the supplier can only inspect the corpse; with it, the cause is usually visible.</p>
<h2>Pro-rata and good faith</h2>
<p>Many warranties reduce the remedy over time. That is normal, but the calculation should be in writing and tied to a measurable value, not to the supplier's discretion. A good warranty also states what happens to the failed pack — return, recycling or disposal — because that cost is part of the claim.</p>

